sec.p:
Statistical Framework: The statistical analyses were performed using both NHST and Bayesian inference. NHST was set at p ≤.05 for omnibus ANOVA tests with post-hoc comparisons with Bonferroni corrections (\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                                                  \usepackage{amsmath}
                                                  \usepackage{wasysym}
                                                  \usepackage{amsfonts}
                                                  \usepackage{amssymb}
                                                  \usepackage{amsbsy}
                                                  \usepackage{mathrsfs}
                                                  \usepackage{upgreek}
                                                  \setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
                                                  \begin{document}$$\frac{\upalpha }{\mathrm{k}}$$\end{document}). Greenhouse–Geisser corrections were applied throughout the analyses for violations of sphericity for repeated-measures ANOVAs (see Lakens et al., 2018). Bayesian analyses were based on an a-priori minimum effect sizes of interest paradigm for lower and higher-bound, such as 40% and 60% for lower and higher-bound for hit rates, and.4 and.6 for sensitivity index A (for thorough reviews, and experimental precedence, on this subject, see Dienes, 2016, 2021a, 2021b; Tsikandilakis et al., 2019a, 2021a). There was no research precedence for individual subliminality, as applied here (see Tsikandilakis et al., 2023b, 2025a, 2026), and timescale analyses applications for conscious versus unconscious processing models (see Jimenez et al., 2025; see also Fisk & Haase, 2025; Fisk, 2026; Haase, 2026). Accordingly, for Bayesian analyses, evidence for the null hypothesis were defined at a Bayes Factor (BF) <.33 using a uniform distribution with priors for a minimum effect size of interest model of BF (H0) at f [0, ≤.05]. Evidence for the alternate hypothesis was defined at BF > 3 using a uniform distribution with priors adjusted for a minimum effect size of interest model of BF (H1) at f [≥.1, + ∞). Intermediate results were considered inconclusive at.33 < BF(H0-1) < 3 (see Eberly & Casella, 2003; pp. 121–127; see also Armstrong et al., 2022; Angers & Biswas, 2003; Dienes, 2021a, 2021b; for statistical parameters, see Fig. 2, Tables 2 and 3 & Appendix 1–3). All Bayes factors were calculated and can be replicated using the online calculator https://harry-tattan-birch.shinyapps.io/bayes-factor-calculator/ (see Chen et al., 2023). Sensitivity index A was used as an objective index of participants’ perceptual performance. It was used because A, compared to d’, is a non-parametric index, and does not involve any requirements or assumptions for the shape of the underlying signal-to-noise likelihood distributions. Furthermore, sensitivity index A involves diagonal Euclidean corrections for scores that lie in the upper left quadrant of the ROC space (i.e., False Alarms Rate ≤.5 & Hit Rate ≥.5) that are not included in non-parametric indices A’ and A'’ (see Zhang & Mueller, 2005; pp. 203–7). This statistical framework was applied throughout the experiments in this paper (see Tsikandilakis et al., 2021a, 2023b, 2025a).Fig. 2
Experimental outcomes, experiment one. In A., B., C. and D. signal-detection performance for SIAA and HRA adjustments; data are presented with stimulus-type specific error bars indicating ± 2 SDs of each measure of each mean, or count. In E., participants’ responses per signal detection item (TP, TN, FP and FN) for SIAA and HRA adjustments.* (Asterisk) shows Bonferroni-corrected significance at p ≤.001, and Bayesian evidence for the alternate at B > 3. H0 (superscript) signifies evidence for equivalence of significance to the null (see Dienes, 2021a; 2021b)
